 # Keys & Trust: Signing, Escrow, Binding, and Separation of Duties
+
+## Request-proof keys: proposed foundation (2026-10-15)
+
+No production route enables this extension. Repository commit trust, key custody,
+and standard Spaces signatures remain unchanged.
+
+### Keep client possession separate from service identity
+
+The client proof uses an ephemeral credential-bound P-256 `did:key`. A required
+service assertion uses a separately published service DID verification method.
+It binds the exact proof digest, authority, route, and lifetime. No new mandatory
+DID key purpose or historical-key fallback is introduced. Service identity still
+requires separate authorization. Neither identity nor possession proves human consent.
+
+### Verify rotation and resolver bounds before integration
+
+The injected adapter forces trusted DID resolution for every assertion and adds
+no cache. The document ID, exact full method ID, and controller must match the
+expected issuer. Removed methods fail after refreshed resolution.
+
+Production must verify resolver network bounds and upstream freshness. Any later
+cache needs finite age and capacity, explicit invalidation, and a rotation
+visibility window. Stale-on-error acceptance is not permitted.
+
 
